/-
# Counting a million matchings and getting zero

The matrix in this example has exactly 1,048,576 perfect matchings. A randomized
algorithm for counting them can return zero or 1,099,511,627,776. It never
returns 1,048,576. Its mean is exactly right.

Run `haze permanent_signs.hz` to get the distributions. All checks use the
integers and exact rational probabilities; this file does not run or check
either VP/VNP proof.
-/

/-
## Two choices per pair

Take a square matrix of zeros and ones. A one means that a row can be paired
with that column. The permanent counts how many ways there are to pair every
row with a different column. On a two-by-two matrix there are only two terms:
`ad + bc`. The determinant uses `ad - bc`. On a block of four ones the permanent
is two and the determinant is zero.

![A block diagonal matrix with two pairs of rows. Within each pair, the rows can take the columns in either order; all entries between pairs are zero.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/permanent-pairs.svg)
Two blocks give four matchings. Twenty blocks give a 40 by 40 matrix with 2^20 matchings.

The [Anthropic preprint](https://github.com/anthropics/formal-math/tree/main/vp-vnp)
that circulated [on X](https://x.com/Liam06972452/status/2124990562906304556)
and [Hacker News](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50620102), and a
[separate OpenAI preprint](https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/VP-and-VNP-are-different-November-23-2026),
claim VP ≠ VNP. Their lower bounds concern circuits that must compute the
permanent on arbitrary inputs. This particular matrix is easy. We know the
answer before trying a shortcut that uses a determinant.
-/

def perm2(a : Num, b : Num, c : Num, d : Num) : Num := a*d + b*c
def det2(a : Num, b : Num, c : Num, d : Num) : Num := a*d - b*c

#eval perm2(1, 1, 1, 1)
#eval det2(1, 1, 1, 1)

/-
## Give each one a random sign

Leave the zeros alone. Give every one an independent sign, either +1 or -1
with equal probability. Compute the determinant of the signed matrix and square
it. This is Godsil and Gutman's estimator for a zero-one permanent.

```signs
+1  +1
+1  +1
```

Changing the bottom right sign from +1 to -1 sends `ad - bc` from zero to
-2. Squaring gives four. Of the sixteen ways to fill the block, eight give
zero and eight give four. The average is two. In Haze the four calls to
`sign()` are separate draws; `square` gets the value of one determinant:

```haze
def fresh() : Num := square(det2(sign(), sign(), sign(), sign()))
```

The same averaging trick works for a general zero-one matrix. Expand the
squared determinant and pick two *different* matchings. At least one edge
belongs to just one of them. Flipping that edge's sign negates their cross
term, so the two signs cancel in the average. A matching paired with itself
contributes one. Adding up those ones gives the permanent.
-/

def sign() : Num := if flip(1/2) then 1 else -1
def square(x : Num) : Num := x*x
def fresh() : Num := square(det2(sign(), sign(), sign(), sign()))

#eval fresh()
#expect fresh()

/-
## What comes out of the 40 by 40 matrix

The determinant of a block diagonal matrix is the product of its block
determinants. One zero wipes out the product. A nonzero estimate requires all
twenty blocks to give four after squaring. With independent signs the whole
distribution fits on two lines. You can ask the checker for both the
distribution and its mean:

```haze
#eval Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate
#expect Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate
```

| Estimate | Probability |
|---:|---:|
| 0 | 1,048,575 / 1,048,576 |
| 1,099,511,627,776 | 1 / 1,048,576 |

That last number in the left column is 4^20. Divide it by 2^20 and you have
the exact mean, 1,048,576. Haze checks this by multiplying one signed block
at a time. It keeps two possible products after each block instead of storing
all the sign assignments.

Now run the estimator 1,048,576 times independently and take the average.
Each nonzero result adds *one entire permanent* to that average. If you see
one, your answer is exactly right. See two and your answer is double. See none
and your answer is still zero, which happens with probability
`(1 - 1/1048576)^1048576`, about 36.8%. That's a calculation from the one-draw
distribution above, not a count from a simulation.

This sparse example is an old objection to the sign estimator. Costello and Vu
[credit Barvinok for it](https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.1909) in section 2 of
their paper. The zeros between the blocks matter here.
-/

enum Wiring = Independent | Parity

def tied(a : Num, b : Num, c : Num) : Num := square(det2(a, b, c, a*b*c))
def block(w : Wiring) : Num :=
  if w == Independent then fresh() else tied(sign(), sign(), sign())

system Blocks(w : Wiring) {
  var estimate : Num := 1
  step { if estimate != 0 { estimate := estimate * block(w) } }
}

#eval Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate
#expect Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate
#prob Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate != 0

/-
## Save one random bit

Suppose someone implements a block with three coin flips. They draw `a`, `b`
and `c`, then set `d = a*b*c`. Every entry is still a fair sign. Even a test of
*any three* entries gives exactly the independent distribution: if `d` is among
the three observed signs, the other two and `d` determine the missing one.
Every triple occurs once among the eight possible inputs.

But `a*d = a*a*b*c = b*c`. The determinant is zero on every draw. Now even the
mean is wrong. There's a cheap test that does catch this: multiply all four
signs. With independent draws that product is negative half the time. With
the shortcut it's always positive.

In [ACX's forecasting discussion](https://www.astralcodexten.com/p/the-ai-forecasting-agenda),
Scott Alexander considers treating several misses on AI capabilities as one
error about the pace of progress. With the sign generator we can inspect the
dependence directly: four fair signs came from only three random bits.

If all you know about the implementation is that it uses one of these two
generators, you needn't invent odds that the author picked the good one. Leave
both possible and keep the choice fixed across the matrix:

```haze
param wiring : Wiring ~= {Independent: 1, Parity: 1}
```

With these weights Haze gives a lower expected estimate of zero and an upper
one of 1,048,576. Actually toss a fair coin to pick the generator once per
matrix and the mean is 524,288; `lottery` in the file does that experiment.
No coin was tossed in the first version. In the [source](#workbench), try giving
`Parity` weight zero. The two bounds then meet at 1,048,576.
-/

param wiring : Wiring ~= {Independent: 1, Parity: 1}
theorem parity_always_cancels : block(Parity) == 0

#expect Blocks(wiring) |= after[20] estimate
#prob Blocks(wiring) |= after[20] estimate != 0

input lottery : Wiring ~ {Independent: 1/2, Parity: 1/2}
#expect Blocks(lottery) |= after[20] estimate

/-
## An arithmetic circuit for the mean

The determinant has polynomial-size arithmetic circuits. For a zero-one input
with `m` ones, the averaging identity can involve `2^m` signings. Having a small
circuit for each summand doesn't tell you how to compute their exact sum.
An arithmetic circuit for the permanent has to output the polynomial itself.
VP allows polynomial-size circuits for polynomial families of polynomially
bounded degree and number of variables. VNP also allows a sum over all settings
of polynomially many extra Boolean inputs. Outside characteristic two the
permanent is VNP-complete. In characteristic two the plus and minus signs
coincide, so there the permanent equals the determinant; the papers use the
Hamiltonian cycle polynomial for the all-fields statement. None of this settles
Boolean P versus NP. [Lance Fortnow's explanation](https://blog.computationalcomplexity.org/2026/11/vp-vnp.html)
sets out the distinction.

The bad samples here don't rule out a good approximation algorithm.
[Jerrum, Sinclair and Vigoda](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sinclair/perm2.pdf)
gave a randomized polynomial-time approximation scheme for nonnegative
matrices by a different method. For the matrix on this page you wouldn't use
either algorithm. Count the blocks.
-/

#prob Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate == 1048576
